    "row": {
      "id": "019f5736-2c20-7f5a-a2e9-122f5a27be24",
      "document_id": "019f5735-154a-7182-aa08-e8557b11a214",
      "fiscal_year": 2027,
      "department": null,
      "program": "Real Estate Tax",
      "indicator_name": "Real Estate Tax rate",
      "value_actual": 1.12,
      "value_target": null,
      "value_prior_year": 1.1225,
      "unit": "dollars per $100 assessed value",
      "raw_extracted_text": "At the same time, the Board appreciates the emphasis on our workforce, with full funding of the Collective Bargaining Agreements with the County's three unions and equitable increases for non-represented employees that include pay scale adjustments to maintain internal equity between the S, M and A scales. We also value the investment that this budget makes in our facilities, recognizing both the importance of safe, well-maintained infrastructure today and the need for continued investment in the years ahead. The proposed budget allocated just over 51 percent of the County's budget -over $3 billion -to Fairfax County Public Schools. The state has yet to finalize its budget; however, we remain optimistic that our state  partners  will  step  up  to  the  plate  and  increase  their  support  for  K-12  education. In  addition  to operating support, the County's budget strengthens our investment in Schools infrastructure, reflecting our shared challenges of aging facilities and deferred maintenance. Continued collaboration on capital planning and facility renewal will be essential to ensure that both County and School buildings remain safe, functional, and able to support the services our residents and students rely on. The Board's mark -up to the FY 2027 budget builds on the County Executive's proposal by reducing the Real Estate Tax rate by ¼ cent, from the current rate of $1.1225 per $100 of assessed value to $1.12 per $100 of assessed value. At the current tax rate, the impact on the average Real Estate Tax bill would have been $357; with this rate reduction, the average increase is reduced to $337. The Board is acutely aware of the financial pressures and uncertainty many residents continue to face and believes it is important to moderate the impact on homeowners where possible. This reduction is made possible in large part due to the imp act  of  the  Food  and  Beverage Tax on the County's budget, which has begun to deliver on its intended purpose of diversifying our revenue base, lessening our reliance on the Real Estate Tax, and allowing some of the cost of County programs to be shared by visitors, commuters, and others who spend money in Fairfax County but do not pay property taxes here. In developing this mark-up package, the Board also carefully considered the concerns raised by residents about several programs recommended for elimination.",
